This file is byte-identical to Docker's built-in default seccomp profile.
It is committed and referenced explicitly from docker-compose.yml rather
than left implicit, for two reasons:

  1. An implicit default is whatever the host's daemon happens to ship.
     Referencing a file makes the applied policy a reviewed artifact that
     travels with the code and cannot change under the application when a
     host is upgraded.
  2. It gives a starting point for tightening. A profile written from
     scratch is how outages happen; one derived from the default and
     narrowed with evidence is not.

Because it is identical to the default, adopting it changes no runtime
behaviour. Any future narrowing must be driven by syscall tracing of the
real workload (strace or a seccomp profile recorder under load), not by
reasoning about which calls a Python service "should" need. Several
syscall groups in this profile are already gated on capabilities the
containers no longer hold, so they are inert regardless.

Update procedure:
  1. Fetch the default profile for the Docker version being deployed.
  2. Diff it against this file and review each change.
  3. Update this file, the version above and the checksum in one commit.
